@meta
id: 30-11
title: Rename symbol, and what it misses
chapter: 30
type: lesson
skills: rename-symbol
par: 245
minutes: 12
vscode: F2, type the new name, press Enter, trust it
vimrc: set hidden
open: src/domain/order/order.provider.ts

@brief
    ORD-590  OrderProvider.sendReceipt is misnamed. It queues an email;
             it sends nothing itself. Rename it emailReceipt, definition
             and callers.

F2 in an IDE. In vim it is a search across the tree and a substitute
over every file that matched — `:vimgrep` then `:cfdo`, both of which
chapter 19 gave you. That part is easy.

The part that is not easy is that a text search has no idea what a
symbol is. Look at the whole list before you change anything. Two of the
hits are not this method at all:

  · `NotificationService` has its own `sendReceipt`. Different class,
    different job, nothing to do with this ticket.
  · the producer publishes on the routing key `'order.sendReceipt'`.
    Consumers subscribe to that string. Change it and messages stop
    being delivered, silently, in production.

Rename the method and its callers. Those two must read exactly as they
do now when you are finished.

@keys
| `:vimgrep /\<name\>/ path/*.ts` | every whole-word hit, into the quickfix list |
| `:copen` | read the list before you act |
| `:cfdo %s/old/new/g \| update` | substitute through every file in the list |
| `/ge` | the `e` flag: no error when a file has no match |
| `:cfdo` vs `:cdo` | once per file, versus once per hit |

@hints
Search wide first — `:vimgrep /\<sendReceipt\>/ src/**/*.ts` and
`:copen` — so you can see what you are about to break.
--
The notification service is in a different directory, so a narrower
glob leaves it out of the list entirely. The routing key is not: it
lives in the same directory as the code you are renaming.
--
Do the blind rename over the narrowed list, then put the routing key
back with a second pass. `%s/pattern/text/ge` with the `e` flag runs
happily in the files that do not contain it.

@solution
    :vimgrep /\<sendReceipt\>/ src/**/*.ts<Enter>  :copen<Enter>
    :vimgrep /\<sendReceipt\>/ src/domain/order/*.ts<Enter>
    :cfdo %s/\<sendReceipt\>/emailReceipt/g | update<Enter>
    :cfdo %s/'order\.emailReceipt'/'order.sendReceipt'/ge | update<Enter>
    :qa<Enter>

Two mitigations, because there are two different failures.

Wrong scope is a *file* problem here, so the fix is a narrower list.
`src/domain/order/*.ts` cannot reach the notification service. This is
the usual shape: the thing you are renaming lives in one part of the
tree, and the same word elsewhere is somebody else's.

A string that happens to contain the name is not a file problem, and no
glob will save you. Either answer each match with the `c` flag, or —
faster on a big rename — change everything and put the handful of
strings back afterwards. The second pass is cheap because you know
exactly what to look for.

Now the honest comparison. A language server renames by symbol: it
resolves `sendReceipt` to one declaration, finds the references that
actually resolve to it, and leaves the other class and the string alone
without being asked. That is a real advantage and this lesson is what it
saves you.

What it costs: a server that understands your project, which means an
install, a config, an index, and a language it supports. On the machine
you ssh into at 2am there is no server, and `:vimgrep` plus `:cfdo`
still renames a symbol across two hundred files in about four seconds.
Both are worth having. Only one of them is always there.

Whichever you use, read `git diff` before you commit. A rename is the
edit most likely to be right in nine places and quietly wrong in the
tenth.

@start src/domain/order/order.provider.ts
import { EmailProducer } from './order.producer';

export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(protected emailProducer: EmailProducer) {}

  public async confirm(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    await this.sendReceipt(order);

    return order;
  }

  public async sendReceipt(order: Order) {
    return this.emailProducer.publish(order.id);
  }
}

@expected src/domain/order/order.provider.ts
import { EmailProducer } from './order.producer';

export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(protected emailProducer: EmailProducer) {}

  public async confirm(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    await this.emailReceipt(order);

    return order;
  }

  public async emailReceipt(order: Order) {
    return this.emailProducer.publish(order.id);
  }
}

@start src/domain/order/order.resolver.ts
import { OrderProvider } from './order.provider';

export class OrderResolver {
  constructor(protected orderProvider: OrderProvider) {}

  public async resendReceipt(_: unknown, args: { id: string }) {
    const order = await this.orderProvider.findOne(args.id);

    return this.orderProvider.sendReceipt(order);
  }
}

@expected src/domain/order/order.resolver.ts
import { OrderProvider } from './order.provider';

export class OrderResolver {
  constructor(protected orderProvider: OrderProvider) {}

  public async resendReceipt(_: unknown, args: { id: string }) {
    const order = await this.orderProvider.findOne(args.id);

    return this.orderProvider.emailReceipt(order);
  }
}

@start src/domain/order/order.producer.ts
export class EmailProducer {
  public async publish(orderId: string) {
    return this.channel.publish({
      routingKey: 'order.sendReceipt',
      payload: { orderId },
    });
  }
}

@start src/infrastructure/services/notification.service.ts
export class NotificationService {
  public async sendReceipt(customerId: string, receiptUrl: string) {
    return this.push.deliver(customerId, { url: receiptUrl });
  }
}
